    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b7133eaf-c6a4-57f5-9026-d4952db7a3d4",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\n$$\n\nWork on a domain where all displayed expressions are defined.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differential equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\cdot {y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\n$$",
